equations\n${9x + 3y - 6z = 8; - 6x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x - 5y - 15z = 10$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$"] [17592186119591 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{9x + 3y + (-6)z = 8; (-6)x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x + (-5)y + (-15)z = 10\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119527 :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z$.\n${x - y + 3z =-1; 4x - 2z = 1; 3x + y - z =-2$"] [17592186119527 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{1x- 1y + 3z =-1; 4x + 0y- 2z = 1; 3x + 1y- 1z =-2\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 3 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (0 - (-2)) + (-4 + 6) + 3(4 - 0) \\\\\n&= 2 + 2 + 12 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z$, we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{z} =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (0 - 1) + (-8 - 3) - (4 - 0) \\\\\n&=-1 - 11 - 4 \\\\\n&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\frac{D_{z}}{D} = \\frac{-16}{16} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119529 :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2$, find the value of $k$.\n${x + 2y - z =-1; kx + 3y - 2z = 1; 3x - y + 2z = 3$"] [17592186119529 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (6 - 2) - 2(2k + 6) - (-k - 9) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k - 12 + k + 9 \\\\\n&= 1 - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{x} =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= - (6 - 2) - 2(2 + 6) - (-1 - 9) \\\\\n&=-4 - 16 + 10 \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2$. So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{-10}{1 - 3k} &= 2 \\\\\n- 10 &= 2 - 6k \\\\\n6k &= 12 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118678 17592186118679 17592186118680 17592186118681 17592186118682 17592186118690 17592186118691 17592186118692 17592186118693 17592186118694 17592186118696 17592186118697 17592186118698 17592186118699 17592186118700 17592186118786 17592186118787 17592186118788 17592186118789 17592186118790] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118677 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118677 :answer-